Info Blurt #84 =96Stainless Steel Threaded Fasteners =96 =93Galling=94 and =
the need for anti-seize Lubricant

When stainless steel bolts and nuts are used i.e. Flanged joints, MJ joints=
, and Joint Restraints, there is potential for =93galling=94 between the bo=
lt and nut threads unless an anti-seize lubricant is used.  Excessive frict=
ion between the mating threads is the root cause of =93galling=94 that caus=
es the threads within the nut to permanently seize or cold-weld to the exte=
rnal threads of the bolt.

Galling prevents proper assembly of the joint by restricting the necessary =
bolt preload.  Because the use of anti-seize lubricant significantly reduce=
s friction and the chance for galling, the installer is required to use ant=
i-seize thread lubricant during assembly.  This is standard practice and un=
derstood by those familiar with assembly of stainless steel bolts and nuts.

The following acceptable anti-seize thread lubricants are available at fast=
ener stores and/or distributors:


=B7       SAF-T-EZE=AE Anti-Seize

=B7       NEVER-SEEZ=AE Anti-Seize

=B7       PERMATEX=AE Anti-Seize

=B7       LOCTITE=AE Anti-Seize

=B7       MARKAL=AE E-Z Break Anti-Seize

=B7       CRC=AE Anti-Seize

=B7       EVERLUBE=AE 620C MoS2/Graphite Solid Film Lubricant

Future assembly instructions for stainless steel hardware will include the =
label below within each accessory kit:

[cid:image002.jpg@01D6F986.0D03B910]















Consideration is being given to implementing =93Xylan=94 coated nuts in acc=
essory kits to provide for thread lubrication and to prevent galling.




